0.2.1
---
Feature
- QxThemeInfo::accent carries the colour the theme emphasizes with, and
  apply() installs it as QPalette::Highlight with HighlightedText as its pair.
  An emphasis is a pair, not a colour: what sits on the fill is painted from
  HighlightedText, and a glyph that asks that role has to find it computed from
  the fill it is actually on - which the palette's own highlight is not, since
  the bundled families emphasize with five different colours. A sheet cannot
  publish the value: it has the accent substituted in, and QSS does not accept
  highlightedtext in a palette() lookup at all. Invalid for a theme that says
  nothing about emphasis, which stays a legitimate registration: that theme gets
  the engine's own highlight, the same answer an application with no registered
  theme gets. accentColor() answers with the theme's accent before the
  platform's, so it cannot disagree with the palette apply() just installed.
  tst_QxThemeBundle::bundledThemesCarryTheirAccent,
  tst_QxTheme::theAccentReachesThePalette
- QxTheme::applyColorScheme(bool dark) / followSystemColorScheme(), in qxtheme.h:
  the third thing a theme has to say about an application, next to the palette and
  the sheet. A palette describes what the application draws; the color scheme
  describes what the *platform* draws for it, and on Windows 11 the two can
  disagree - the windows11 style that Qt 6.7 made the default paints from its own
  light and dark table chosen by the system scheme and never reads the palette, so
  a light theme on a dark desktop came out with dark input fields, dark scroll
  bars and a dark title bar, and setting a light palette could not reach any of
  it. From Qt 6.8 the scheme can be set (QStyleHints::setColorScheme) and these
  two functions are that call, spelled with the flag a theme is registered with;
  below 6.8, and on platforms that ignore the request, they do nothing. The engine
  installs the scheme before the palette on every apply(), and hands it back to
  the platform while following the system, because a pinned scheme would also
  silence the colorSchemeChanged signal following is built on.
  tst_QxTheme::colorSchemeHintLeavesThePaletteAlone
- QxTheme::tintedPixmap() / tintedIcon() / themedPixmap() / themedIcon(), in
  qxthemeicon.h: a single-colour glyph repainted from the palette the theme
  installed - by role for the themed pair, by an explicit colour for the tinted
  pair. The glyph is rasterised and then repainted through its alpha, so the
  colour the file was drawn in is replaced outright and the antialiased edges
  keep their coverage. Asking by role is the point of the themed pair: the same
  call answers differently on a light theme and a dark one, so an application
  that hands its icons through it follows a theme change by re-asking rather
  than by tracking one. QxRibbon and QxDock keep the routines they already had -
  neither library depends on this one, and pointing them at qxtheme would put it
  under both - so what the three share is the rule in doc/pages/icons.md and the
  gate   that checks it, not an implementation. tst_QxTheme::tintedPixmapRepaintsTheGlyph
- QxThemeIcons: keeps the actions an application hands it painted from the
  palette, and repaints them when the palette is replaced - which is what a
  theme switch does. Painting an icon once fixes its colour, and a fixed colour
  is exactly what a theme switch breaks, so an application that asked once and
  stopped asking was no better off than one that shipped a black glyph. add()
  paints immediately and remembers what it would need to answer again; the
  palette change is watched on the application object, so nothing has to be
  announced by hand, and refresh() is there for a palette replaced in a way Qt
  does not announce. An action destroyed while its icon is being kept up to date
  is dropped rather than painted into. Nothing is repainted unless an
  application asked for it to be. tst_QxTheme::iconsFollowThePalette
- QxThemeInfo::onAccent is the colour to paint on the fill QxThemeInfo::accent
  names. An emphasis is a pair, and the theme is the one that picked it: the
  sheet writes both and was drawn to look right with them together, so a second
  opinion computed from the fill alone is one more place for the two halves to
  disagree - which is how a dock's auto hide buttons came out black on a fill
  the same theme labels white. apply() installs it as QPalette::HighlightedText,
  the pair of the Highlight it already installs; invalid for a theme that says
  nothing about emphasis, which then gets the computed answer as before. An
  accent override replaces the fill, so it replaces the pair with it - a colour
  the theme never saw has no answer of the theme's to go with it.

Bugfix
- A second launch no longer reports a theme it is about to install. The engine
  restored the stored id and applied it from instance(), which is before any
  registration can have happened - registering means calling instance() first -
  so the lookup found an empty table and warned "no theme registered under
  ...", and the first frame wore the palette Qt started with rather than the
  theme's. The restored id is remembered instead, and the registration that
  supplies it is what installs it - which is also the moment an application's
  own order of "restore, then register" reaches the theme it named.
  tst_QxTheme::restoredThemeWaitsForItsRegistration
- The gallery is no longer dressed by the sheet. Its group paints from the
  palette now, and the ::item rules the skeletons carried described a state the
  sheet never delivered: a pseudo-state on a custom class matched nothing, so
  the selected fill they promised was never seen, while the hover border that
  did land moved the item's contents a pixel - the jumping. The rules are gone,
  and the popup's group titles take the hairline caption they never had.
- A button whose menu was open showed nothing. The bar's buttons had a hover
  and no held state, so opening a menu left the button looking idle and the
  menu appeared to come from nowhere; the click has to land somewhere. Pressed
  and open are stated now, following RibbonButton's answer: a light scheme
  outlines the wash with the accent, since a solid accent fill would put a dark
  glyph on a mid-blue box, and a dark scheme fills it outright, every glyph
  there being light already.
- A hover no light theme could show. Every button's hover was filled with
  accent-faint, a wash 93% of the way to white, which on a white surface is
  four levels of difference - a hover the eye has to look for. They take
  accent-soft now, the wash the palette already named for a hover, and the
  one the dock's buttons were given to match. flat-light's sat further out
  than the other families' and was brought to theirs; slate-dark's sat at the
  accent's own lightness and left the glyph on it at 4.0:1, so it was taken
  down to where every other dark family's is.
- Icon buttons were rounded where they have nothing to round: a hover is a wash
  with no box of its own, and a rounded wash reads as a lozenge pasted on the
  bar rather than as the button lighting up. The platform style was choosing
  the radius, because a wash the sheet does not state is its to choose - and on
  a light theme the sheet states far less. The ribbon's icon buttons and the
  quick access bar's are square now - by the bar's type selector, since the
  actions it carries become plain tool buttons the toolbar builds itself and
  the one button the library names is only the customize popup; the gallery,
  the combo and the line edit keep the family's radius, since those do draw
  a box.
- The light scheme's menus came out at whatever height the platform style
  hands out, because the light skeleton carried no menu rules at all: the
  geometry both schemes state now lives in menu.css, which the generator puts
  ahead of each skeleton, and the dark skeleton's menu fixes are in it too -
  every item filled with the separator colour made the rows read as blocks
  that moved under the pointer, and a selected row filled with the surface
  looked like a hole punched through them. The item carries no fill and the
  selected row is the one thing that lifts off; the bar's items get a stated
  box, which is also what makes the bar's height the sheet's answer.
- readableOn() picked the wrong half of the pair on a saturated blue. It asked
  which of black and white is further from the fill, and on #3a96dd - the accent
  two of the bundled families emphasize with - black is: 6.6:1 against white's
  3.2:1. So the palette handed out QPalette::HighlightedText as black while the
  same theme's sheet writes text-on-fill as white, and every glyph that asks the
  role - a dock's auto hide buttons, a focused tab's close button - came out
  black on the very fill it is supposed to read against. It now measures WCAG
  contrast and prefers white, taking black only where white cannot reach the
  3:1 a user interface element is held to, which is the answer those themes
  give. Themes are no longer asked to agree by computation: QxThemeInfo::onAccent
  carries the colour itself.

0.2.0
---
Feature
- systemAccentColor() reports the accent colour the platform uses, or an
  invalid colour when it does not name one. The engine could already read the
  system colour scheme, but not the accent, which lived in a private header of
  qxwindow and was reachable from one drawing call. From Qt 6.6 on it is
  QPalette::Accent - one piece of code, every platform - and below that only
  Windows keeps it somewhere readable, in the DWM key of the registry, which is
  the single platform branch here; elsewhere the answer is that the platform
  does not say.
- QxThemeManager::accentColor() / setAccentColor() are the colour in force and
  the override that replaces it, with accentColorChanged() to follow. The
  platform's colour is taken once, in instance(), before the first apply() -
  after that the application palette belongs to the theme and asking the
  platform would be asking the engine about its own work. Only an override
  reaches the palette: a theme carries a highlight colour of its own and was
  designed around it, so honouring the platform accent by default would repaint
  every theme. The accompanying text colour is picked for contrast. The
  override is stored beside the theme and removed, not stored invalid, when it
  is cleared. tst_QxTheme::accentColor

Interface
- QxThemeManager addresses a theme by id - a QString - and registers none of its
  own. The Theme enumeration is gone, and with it themeId(), themeName(),
  themeFromId(), isDarkTheme(), themeStyleSheetPath() and themeStyleSheet(); so
  is the table behind them, which named the ribbon's style sheets and mirrored
  the ribbon's own enumeration. Which themes exist is the application's to say:
  it hands QxThemeManager::registerTheme() a QxThemeInfo - an id, a name,
  whether the theme is dark, the style sheet - and picks one with setTheme(id).
  theme(), systemLightTheme() and systemDarkTheme() return the id and
  themeChanged() carries it, while themeInfo(), themeIds(), hasTheme() and
  unregisterTheme() answer what was registered. What the engine keeps is what
  was never about a particular theme: persistence, following the system, the
  accent colour and the two palettes. An id written by an earlier version -
  "dark-wps" and the rest - still restores, because ids were always what was
  stored.
- palette(Theme) becomes paletteFor(bool dark): with no table to look a theme up
  in, brightness is the only thing left to ask for a palette by.
- setStyleSheetFile() is gone. A theme carries its style sheet in its
  registration, so reading one from a file is the application's line to write;
  setStyleSheet() now takes an id and replaces the sheet of that registration.

Cleanup
- Drop the Qt 5.12 guard around QPalette::PlaceholderText in buildPalette(),
  which both the light and the dark scheme go through. The role exists since
  5.12, below the 5.15 minimum, so the placeholder colour is now set
  unconditionally.

0.1.1
---
Bugfix
- QX_THEME_DEPRECATED_SINCE(major, minor) never answered true for a deprecation
  that had already happened: QX_THEME_DISABLE_DEPRECATED_BEFORE defaulted to
  QX_THEME_VERSION, so the deprecation point was compared against the version
  being built - (0,1,0) > (0,1,0) is false - and the answer flipped back to false
  on every minor bump. The default is now a fixed floor, so an API stays declared
  for as long as it is inside its removal buffer (K26), and raising the cutoff is
  what compiles it out. tst_qxdeprecated covers all seven libraries
